    description: "The self-audit (docs/hypergumbo.claims.yaml + docs/hypergumbo-self-catalog/, gated by scripts/check-self-claims, published in SECURITY.md) covers hypergumbo-core's CLI only: its sources are the core cmd_* handlers. hypergumbo-tracker appears only as a \"dev zone\" SINK (claims assert the analysis commands never reach tracker code). Nothing audits the tracker package as a product, although it ships on PyPI (`htrac`): `htrac serve` (Starlette/uvicorn HTTP + WebSocket server with a write API), `sync` (git branch/commit/push, GitHub/Forgejo API), `tui`, the CLI, the stop hook, embeddings.\n\nAdd tracker self-claims in the same machinery: declare the htrac entry points (serve handlers, sync, tui, CLI subcommands) as sources; state per-entry-point claims (e.g. CLI/tui: writes only under the tracker dirs and journal, no network; sync: network only to the configured forge, subprocess only git; serve: binds only to the configured address, every mutating route requires an authenticated session); extend the snapshot (docs/hypergumbo.claims.expected.json) and SECURITY.md generation; make check-self-claims cover it. Expect `violated` on serve until WI-hopip lands (serve.py's write API has no auth; the auth modules are imported by nothing) -- record that verdict honestly rather than waiting. Owner request 2026-10-07 (comfort installing hypergumbo on a personal daily-driver machine and recommending it)."  # 187b
    fields: {}  # 187b
